{
  "semantics": {
    "attributes": {
      "final": {},
      "header": {}
    },
    "blocks": [
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "STOMP"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "link",
            "target": "https://stomp.github.io/stomp-specification-1.2.html#Abstract"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " (Simple Text Oriented Messaging Protocol) was originally created for scripting languages (such as Ruby, Python, and Perl) to connect to enterprise message brokers. It is designed to address a minimal subset of commonly used messaging patterns. STOMP can be used over any reliable two-way streaming network protocol, such as TCP and WebSocket. Although STOMP is a text-oriented protocol, message payloads can be either text or binary."
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "STOMP is a frame-based protocol whose frames are modeled on HTTP. The following listing shows the structure of a STOMP frame:"
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "listing",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "COMMAND\nheader1:value1\nheader2:value2\n\nBody^@"
          }
        ],
        "language": null,
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "Clients can use the "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "SEND"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " or "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "SUBSCRIBE"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " commands to send or subscribe for messages, along with a "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "destination"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " header that describes what the message is about and who should receive it. This enables a simple publish-subscribe mechanism that you can use to send messages through the broker to other connected clients or to send messages to the server to request that some work be performed."
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "When you use Spring’s STOMP support, the Spring WebSocket application acts as the STOMP broker to clients. Messages are routed to "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "@Controller"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " message-handling methods or to a simple in-memory broker that keeps track of subscriptions and broadcasts messages to subscribed users. You can also configure Spring to work with a dedicated STOMP broker (such as RabbitMQ, ActiveMQ, and others) for the actual broadcasting of messages. In that case, Spring maintains TCP connections to the broker, relays messages to it, and passes messages from it down to connected WebSocket clients. Thus, Spring web applications can rely on unified HTTP-based security, common validation, and a familiar programming model for message handling."
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "The following example shows a client subscribing to receive stock quotes, which the server may emit periodically (for example, via a scheduled task that sends messages through a "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "SimpMessagingTemplate"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " to the broker):"
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "listing",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "SUBSCRIBE\nid:sub-1\ndestination:/topic/price.stock.*\n\n^@"
          }
        ],
        "language": null,
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "The following example shows a client that sends a trade request, which the server can handle through an "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "@MessageMapping"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " method:"
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "listing",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "SEND\ndestination:/queue/trade\ncontent-type:application/json\ncontent-length:44\n\n{\"action\":\"BUY\",\"ticker\":\"MMM\",\"shares\",44}^@"
          }
        ],
        "language": null,
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "After the execution, the server can broadcast a trade confirmation message and details down to the client."
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "The meaning of a destination is intentionally left opaque in the STOMP spec. It can be any string, and it is entirely up to STOMP servers to define the semantics and the syntax of the destinations that they support. It is very common, however, for destinations to be path-like strings where "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "/topic/.."
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " implies publish-subscribe (one-to-many) and "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "/queue/"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " implies point-to-point (one-to-one) message exchanges."
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "STOMP servers can use the "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "MESSAGE"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " command to broadcast messages to all subscribers. The following example shows a server sending a stock quote to a subscribed client:"
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "listing",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "MESSAGE\nmessage-id:nxahklf6-1\nsubscription:sub-1\ndestination:/topic/price.stock.MMM\n\n{\"ticker\":\"MMM\",\"price\":129.45}^@"
          }
        ],
        "language": null,
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "A server cannot send unsolicited messages. All messages from a server must be in response to a specific client subscription, and the "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "subscription"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " header of the server message must match the "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "id"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " header of the client subscription."
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "The preceding overview is intended to provide the most basic understanding of the STOMP protocol. We recommended reviewing the protocol "
          },
          {
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "specification"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "link",
            "target": "https://stomp.github.io/stomp-specification-1.2.html"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " in full."
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      }
    ],
    "outline": null,
    "title": [
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "value": "Overview"
      }
    ]
  }
}
